        {
          "chunk_id": "med-chunk-efcd1c95f5fdcb645f93",
          "document_id": "med-doc-e394863eadce10631150",
          "domain": "medical",
          "text": "the U.S. adult population. In 2012, about 86 million people in the U.S. had pre-diabetes, and 51% of those 65 or older had prediabetes. Learn more about prediabetes. Gestational Diabetes Some women develop diabetes during the late stages of pregnancy. This is called gestational diabetes. Although this form of diabetes usually goes away after the baby is born, a woman who has had it has a lifelong risk for developing diabetes, mostly type 2.",
          "score": 0.012909035409035409,
          "source_url": "https://github.com/abachaa/MedQuAD/tree/577bd37b96c02d1833b2c9eed2de9f96964e96cb",
          "locator": "med-doc-e394863eadce10631150/med-chunk-efcd1c95f5fdcb645f93",
          "text_sha256": "9c7c15edb4acd3dc4b0a0747346902f7d9c45bcc98da9c55a47c02dfae7d620e",
          "source_sha256": "42f025f955852567436dc7cd7efde701ca014429f47dacae7d7c0ba23ab51f29",
          "graph_path": [
            "kg-node-fcb21efed59ffe4ef1ceac5c",
